{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>All My plagues.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): He called the plague of hail 'all My plagues' because in the plague of hail many plagues were included upon man and upon beast — this is what is meant by 'from man unto beast,' and the breaking of all the trees, and needless to say the destruction of the grain and the fruit and all the good of the land, from the extremity of the cold of the hail. And thus David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 78:47): ״ושקמותם בחנמל״ — this is a species of hail, and it is the strong frost that breaks the blossoms of the trees and dries the moisture. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: Why is it called חנמל? Because it would come and rest upon the sycamores and circumcise (מל) them — how much more so this hail, in which fire was kindled within it." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'All My plagues' — the hail would strike and shatter the trees, and the fire would burn the roots; and the cold of the hail would not weaken the power of the fire, nor would the power of the fire consume the cold of the hail. To what may the matter be compared? To a king, blessed be He, who has two dukes who hate one another. In time the king went out to war and had need of them, so he made peace between them. Thus it is written (Job 25:2): ״המשל ופחד עמו עושה שלום במרומיו״ — 'dominion and fear are with Him; He makes peace in His heights.' 'Dominion' — this is Michael; 'fear' — this is Gabriel. Michael is of the water, Gabriel is of the fire, and the Holy One, blessed be He, makes peace between them." "<b>And likewise</b> we find in the matter of the Chariot that Ezekiel saw, for it is written (Ezekiel 1:13): ״ודמות החיות מראיהם כגחלי אש״, and it is written (ibid.): ״ודמות על ראשי החיה רקיע״ and so forth — the nature of water is to descend below and the nature of fire is to rise above, and the Holy One, blessed be He, placed the fire below and the firmament of water above." "<b>And you will find</b> in this parashah fourteen occurrences of 'hail' (ברד), and the reason for this is that it is of the force of water, and it is known that the sphere of the moon moves the element of water just as the sphere of the sun moves the element of fire. From this it follows that rivers and streams increase with the moon's waxing and diminish with its waning. Now the moon waxes for fourteen days; from there onward it wanes and diminishes." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'All My plagues' — like ״ולא יעיר כל חמתו״, in which כל שהיא חמתו (all that is His wrath); so too here, כל מגפותי — כל שהיא מגפתי (all that is My plague), for מגפותי is written deficient in waw: מגפתי. And it says: 'upon your heart' — against your heart, that is, the strength of your stubborn heart; and He informed him that He would humble his strong heart through the measure of כל שהיא מגפתו (all that is His plague). And Moses our master, peace be upon him, needed in this plague to pray with lifting of hands, which was not so in all the others, for the reason that people were dying in it from the terror of the thunder and from the plague of hail, which came about with the agreement of the court on high, as it is written (Exodus 9:23): ״וה׳ נתן קולות וברד״. Therefore, in order to annul them, he needed to lift up his hands, so that they would be sanctified all ten, and so that he would direct his intention in them toward the height of heaven — for Pharaoh now acknowledged Him — and the purpose of lifting his hands toward Him was in order to draw down from above and cause flow below, to interrupt the punishment and to have mercy upon the earth. And this is what Moses said: 'I will spread my hands unto HaShem' — that is, I will spread my hands corresponding to the ten, and they will be sanctified, and I will direct my intention in them unto HaShem, and immediately the thunder will cease." "<b>So that you may know that there is none like Me in all the earth.</b> He informed him, in the general thrust of His words, of the matter of the existence of the Blessed Name and of providence and of power — for He, blessed be He, exists, and exercises providence, and is able. He exists: this is what He mentioned in the plague of the frogs, ״למען תדע כי אין כה׳״ — 'so that you may know that there is none like YHWH' — that is, there is no other existence among beings like His existence. He exercises providence: this is what He said in the plague of the wild beasts, ״למען תדע כי אני ה' בקרב הארץ״ — 'so that you may know that I am YHWH in the midst of the land' — that is, exercising providence like a king within the province, by being close to the extremities. He is able: this is what He mentioned here in the plague of the hail, ״בעבור תדע כי אין כמוני בכל הארץ״ — 'so that you may know that there is none like Me in all the earth' — that is, that there is no possessor of power like Me. And Moshe mentioned thus to Pharaoh because Pharaoh denied all three — His existence, His providence, and His power." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ״בעבור תדע״ — 'so that you may know.' You said, ״לא ידעתי את ה׳״ — 'I do not know YHWH'; therefore He mentions to him at every turn ״למען תדע״ — 'so that you may know.' ״בעבור תדע״ — 'so that you may know' — for each and every matter He recalls his sin. Thus it is written (Proverbs 27): ״אם תכתוש את האויל במכתש בתוך הריפות בעלי וגו׳״ — 'Though you pound the fool in a mortar with a pestle among crushed grain, etc.' — in the midst of his being pounded, when the pestle is lifted from the mortar, he forgets the blows; so too Pharaoh the wicked — between the poundings of the plagues, from one time to the next, would strengthen his heart."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {1 "The phrase מלך ב\"ו appears to abbreviate מלך ברוך הוא ('a king, blessed be He'), conflating the parable's king with the Holy One, blessed be He; resolved accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:57:33.792516146Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}